TokenFlow on Columbia Insomnia
=============================

A student uses an OpenAI client, an inference key, a model alias and a gateway URL
ending in /v1. The operator owns the service host, Slurm account, checkpoint,
container, and trusted network route. See README.md for the student example and
docs/PLATFORM.md and docs/PROVIDERS.md for the operator APIs. A permanent service
host requires site approval. The procedure here uses temporary CPU compute jobs
for the controllers and separate GPU compute jobs for the engines.

Student access and pilot readiness
----------------------------------

An operator defines a pool with a checkpoint, backend, GPU type, TP width,
capacity and maximum replica count. Students select its alias when sending
an inference request. The controller allocates and discovers a worker within
those bounds. Students can use examples/student_chat.py with OPENAI_BASE_URL
and OPENAI_API_KEY and wait for a cold pool. An inference key grants access
to inference routes. Pool limits and worker management require an admin key.

For a course pilot, establish who owns the Slurm allocation, who may use each
model, where the HTTPS service runs, how keys are issued and revoked, and who
responds to queue, storage and startup failures. The operator's Slurm account
owns the batch jobs. The local end-to-end test and the Insomnia proof below
do not exercise campus identity, per-student GPU accounting, or a permanent
multi-user service. A single gateway process owns each deployment's registry.

1. Connect once from Windows
----------------------------

Connect the Columbia VPN. Open Ubuntu WSL in Windows Terminal. Check the
cluster host key with your trusted Columbia host-key record on initial login.
Enter your password and Duo only in the SSH prompt. Keep the control socket
private. No credentials need to be copied to another agent.

    login=YOUR_UNI@insomnia.rcs.columbia.edu
    session=$(mktemp -d /tmp/tokenflow-ssh.XXXXXXXX)
    sock="$session/ctl"
    ssh -M -S "$sock" -o ControlPersist=10m -o ServerAliveInterval=60 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 -fN "$login"
    ssh -S "$sock" -O check "$login"
    ssh -S "$sock" -o BatchMode=yes "$login" 'hostname'

Run the commands that use these variables in the same WSL shell. Windows
OpenSSH and Ubuntu OpenSSH use separate known_hosts files. If Windows has the
verified entry, use its file as UserKnownHostsFile or copy that file into the
private session directory before connecting. The socket reuses your existing
authenticated transport. A disconnection or VPN expiry can require Duo again.
Checking the queue every five minutes keeps this session in use and shows
progress. Submitted batch jobs continue if SSH disconnects.

2. Check prerequisites on Insomnia
----------------------------------

On a login shell, keep commands short. Heavy work and long-running gateways
belong in allocations. For noninteractive SSH, use the site's login environment
with bash -lc. On the installation tested here SLURM_CONF=/etc/slurm/slurm.conf.

    squeue --me
    sinfo -N -p burst -o '%N %G %t'
    sacctmgr -nP show assoc where user="$USER" format=account,qos
    ls -lh "$HOME/vllm-runtime/containers/vllm.sif"
    ls "$HOME/vllm-runtime/models/qwen2.5-14b-instruct/config.json"

This sweep uses account edu, partition/QoS burst, one node per worker and
GRES gpu:A6000:N. The inspected nodes advertised up to eight A6000s each.
Other advertised GPUs and counts vary. Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct has 40 query heads
and 8 KV heads, compatible with TP sizes 1, 2, 4 and 8. Allocation depends on
availability of simultaneous devices on a single node. Inspect the actual job
with scontrol show job JOBID. Slurm's prospective start times can change.

The real 0.5B lifecycle test used Slurm GPU job 14123626. TokenFlow discovered a
healthy worker, streamed six content chunks, received GPU telemetry, then set
the pool limits to zero. Slurm recorded cancellation from the intentional drain.
The first 14B probe hit its 1,000-second readiness deadline during weight loading.
Allow for shared-storage reads, Apptainer unpacking and kernel compilation.

3. Stage one reproducible snapshot
----------------------------------

From your local WSL TokenFlow checkout, use a fresh campaign directory on
the shared edu project filesystem. It is visible to login and compute nodes.
The examples use git archive so the published files are exactly the commit you
select. Use a commit containing the managed sweep scripts listed below.

    uni=${login%@*}
    campaign=/insomnia001/depts/edu/users/$uni/tp-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)
    ssh -S "$sock" "$login" "umask 077 && mkdir '$campaign'"
    git archive --format=tar.gz HEAD src experiments/hpc_tp_controller.py experiments/hpc_tp_load.py experiments/vllm_metrics.py scripts/hpc-tp-controller.sh > "$session/source.tgz"
    scp -o ControlPath="$sock" "$session/source.tgz" "$login:$campaign/"
    ssh -S "$sock" "$login" "cd '$campaign' && tar xzf source.tgz && bash -n scripts/hpc-tp-controller.sh"
    git rev-parse HEAD

Record that commit, pool configuration and actual allocation alongside results.
Keep credential files, private SQLite state and worker job directories private.
Keep model weights in existing storage and set job caches to node-local TMPDIR.
An image that reports missing squashfuse will unpack the SIF on each cold start.
A 14B checkpoint on a busy shared filesystem can take many minutes to read.

4. Submit and observe
---------------------

In an authenticated Insomnia login shell, cd to the staged campaign. Each
controller needs python3.12 and ensurepip in the host compute environment,
Slurm clients, project storage and a route to compute-node worker ports.
Controller packages install in node-local storage. On nodes whose system
Python cannot load sqlite3_deserialize, the controller uses a job-local SQLite
wheel. Some nodes lack python3.12 entirely. If one of those is selected use
--nodes=1 --nodelist=<verified-control-node> on the CPU submission. ins043
supported the interpreter in the November 2026 sweep.

Example for one TP size and one engine sequence cap:

    sbatch --parsable --output=control-%j.log scripts/hpc-tp-controller.sh 4 64

The first number sets GPUs used by one vLLM engine. The second configures
vLLM max-num-seqs and TokenFlow admission capacity. Repeat for TP 1, 2, 4, 8
and caps 16, 64. Use --dependency=afterany:CONTROLLER_ID to run the second cap
after the first for that width. Check the existing submission ledger before
retrying. Each controller uses an isolated namespace and provisions at most one
GPU worker. Fresh source snapshots and controller job IDs give independent
directories. A controller can wait for a GPU allocation; it uses CPU resources
while waiting. The queued worker and the CPU controller have different job IDs.

    squeue --me -o '%i %T %M %R %j'
    squeue --start --me
    tail -n 12 control-CONTROLLER_ID.log
    cat results/CONTROLLER_ID/workers/*/job-id
    sacct -X -j CONTROLLER_ID,GPU_JOB_ID --format=JobID,State,Elapsed,AllocTRES,End

The gateway binds an OS-assigned loopback port on its CPU node. The Slurm
provider submits the worker with a derived credential, runs Apptainer, and
publishes its port after the engine returns the expected alias. The gateway
registers the private node address and polls health, engine metrics and the
adjacent GPU exporter. The controller waits for a real telemetry sample,
captures topology, runs the load through the gateway, sets both pool limits
to zero and verifies the worker disappeared from Slurm and the registry.

5. Evidence and interpretation
-----------------------------

The default workload uses Qwen 14B BF16, the same seeded synthetic prompts,
a 2,048-token context limit and 256 output tokens per completed request.
Client concurrency is 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 and additionally 32 and 64 for the
64-sequence engine. The scheduler dynamically batches active requests.
Offered concurrency, configured maximum sequences and observed batch size
are distinct. Engine running/waiting counts describe sampled active load.
Three shuffled repetitions per cell follow two fixed warmups. A cell runs
for at least 30 seconds and launches at least max(12, 2*concurrency) requests.
Trials within a configuration share a warm engine and its release defaults.

    results/CONTROLLER_ID/config.json                pool and provisioner options
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/checkpoint.json            checkpoint JSON hashes
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/worker.json                registered device IDs and engine version
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/topology.txt               physical link matrix
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/gpu.csv                    UTC node samples every ~500 ms
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/engine.jsonl.gz            epoch-stamped raw Prometheus and parsed gauges
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/trial-*/c-*/requests.jsonl individual streamed requests
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/trial-*/c-*/summary.json   complete cell time window and metrics
    results/CONTROLLER_ID/release.json               verified drain and Slurm accounting

GPU CSV columns are timestamp, index, UUID, GPU utilization %, memory-controller
utilization %, used/total MiB, watts, SM/memory clocks in MHz and temperature C.
Only allocated UUIDs are sampled. Join UTC sample times to start_epoch and
end_epoch. Keep startup and post-run idle separate from in-cell idle.
Utilization estimates the fraction of time with a kernel executing. Neither
sampled idle time nor throughput establishes memory, compute or communication
bottlenecks. TTFT is first visible content. TPOT is approximate request-level
time per output token; SSE chunks can contain more than one token. Compare
matched cells at equal load, engine cap, prompt, model, and topology. Report
coverage and failed or unobserved cells alongside any comparison.

6. Download, diagnose and finish
--------------------------------

Copy results with the same SSH socket. Save large traces to a drive with room.
For an incremental WSL copy, create a private local directory and use rsync:

    dest=/mnt/d/TokenFlow-experiments/$(basename "$campaign")
    mkdir -p "$dest"
    rsync -rt --partial -e "ssh -S $sock -o BatchMode=yes" --exclude=workers --exclude='state.sqlite*' --exclude=src --exclude='*.tgz' "$login:$campaign/" "$dest/"

For an attended campaign, the local watcher checks Slurm accounting and copies
updates every five minutes. Keep WSL, VPN and the laptop running for local
monitoring. Reconnect and restart the watcher after a network interruption.

    bash scripts/hpc-watch-tp.sh "$login" "$sock" "$campaign" "$dest"
    python experiments/summarize_hpc_tp.py "$dest"
    python experiments/audit_hpc_tp.py "$dest"

The summary CSV has one row per measured cell and allocated GPU. Request
throughput is repeated across the device rows for that cell. Idle seconds are
estimated only across sampled intervals up to two seconds apart. Consult the
coverage fraction, engine sample count, and separate utilization percentage.

On failure inspect the CPU and worker logs, the Slurm accounting states and
the last completed per-cell summary. A registered resource, a passed warmup
or a zero exit code alone cannot verify a complete sweep. A logically complete
configuration has 15 cells at cap 16 or 21 at cap 64, with successful raw requests,
both telemetry sources during each cell and a confirmed release.

If a controller is interrupted, check its exact job IDs and scoped worker name
using its prefix and job-id receipt. Cancel only the jobs belonging to that test.
Confirm workers and controller are absent from squeue and have terminal accounting
records. Preserve raw output across retries. Close the temporary SSH connection
after the last status check and transfer.

    ssh -S "$sock" -O exit "$login"

November 27, 2026 campaign handoff
---------------------------------

The campaign directory is insomnia-live-20261127t062025-sweep under the
operator's edu project directory. The local copy uses the same basename under
D:\TokenFlow-experiments. The private one-GPU pilot evidence is in the local
TokenFlow checkout under experiments/results/insomnia-live-20261127t062025/lifecycle.jsonl.
All campaign times used for analysis are UTC. Slurm displays cluster local time.

Width        cap 64 controller     cap 16 controller
TP=1         14123705              14123706
TP=2         14123717              14123718
TP=4         14123709              14123710
TP=8         14123711              14123712

Controllers 14123707 and 14123708 exited before provisioning on a CPU node
without python3.12. Their replacements run on ins043. For the first wave,
the controller originally submitted 150-minute, 96-GiB, 12-CPU GPU requests.
The pending jobs were adjusted to 80 minutes and eight requested CPUs. TP=1
and TP=2 were further adjusted to 48 GiB and a 60-minute minimum for backfill.
Insomnia can allocate more CPUs when requested memory exceeds the per-CPU
limit. Use the actual Slurm resource record when reporting the experiment.
The local watcher records latest-slurm.txt every five minutes; submissions.txt
is the durable list of attempts. Read current status from those files or Slurm.

The cap-64 TP=2 run completed 21 timed cells and 1,276 successful streams.
The verifier matched its raw output tokens and telemetry and found the drain
receipt for GPU job 14123723. TP=1 controller 14123705 stopped on an HTTP
429 in the first trial after two completed cells. Controller 14123972 is a
fresh cap-64 TP=1 attempt. The revised runner preserves individual admission
failures and continues a cell while other requests succeed. Compare failure
rates when interpreting throughput. A verified cell can include accurately
accounted failed requests.
